Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. 
Dispute 21 - Day 14:

Furnisher Duties: 
The Obligations of Information Furnishers When Reporting to a CRA & How
to Avoid Getting Frivolous Dispute Responses

Bruce, in the previous lesson we learned exactly what, when and
how a furnisher is required to report consumer account information to a
credit reporting agency (CRA). In today's lesson, we are going to take a
look at the steps a furnisher must follow when being notified of a
consumer dispute through a Credit Reporting Agency as well as when the
dispute is directly with the furnisher.   

Please note, the information covered in lessons 12, 13, and 14 are
Furnisher Rules and Guidelines established by the Federal Trade
Commission. The FTC enforces the legal obligations of the furnisher and
has guidance about the furnisher's responsibilities to report
information that's accurate and complete, and to investigate consumer
disputes about the accuracy of information they provide. The information
we will discuss in today's lesson comes directly from the FTC 

see FCRA 

Consumers may dispute information that is reporting in two ways: 

1. They may submit a dispute to the CRA. 
2. They may submit a dispute directly to the Furnisher reporting the
information on their credit report. 

Furnisher Duties when Disputing with a CRA

When you send an "indirect dispute" to the CRA (bureau, Lexisnexis,
etc), once that CRA notifies the furnisher of the dispute, the furnisher
must: 

● investigate the dispute and review all relevant information provided
by the CRA about the dispute;
Bruce, the problem here lies in the bureaus 2nd line of defense,
e-OSCAR. The Law states the CRA must provide the furnisher with all
relevant information for rewiew. What if this relevant information
contains bills, statements, or other documentation pertinent to the
dispute? e-Oscar prevents the sharing of this information...

● report your findings to the CRA;
Remember, this is done using the Automated Consumer Dispute Verification
form (ACDV)

● provide corrected information to every CRA that received the
information if your investigation shows the information is incomplete or
inaccurate; and
How is it that when one bureau deletes information the others don't
automatically follow suit if, by law, this is the duty of the furnisher.

● modify the information, delete it, or permanently block its
reporting if the information turns out to be inaccurate or incomplete or
can't be verified. 

FCRA 623(b)(1) 

The furnisher must complete these steps within the same time allowed
under the FCRA for the CRA to resolve the dispute. Normally, this is 30
days after the CRA gets the dispute from the consumer. If the consumer
provides additional relevant information during the 30 day period, the
CRA has 15 more days to resolve the dispute. This is why you should
never continuously send in a dispute for the same account within a 30
day period. Aside being risked as marked as frivolous, you will likely
extend the time period the bureau/furnisher has to complete the
investigation - even it the dispute was for another reason. 

The CRA must give the furnisher all the relevant information it gets
within five business days of receipt, and must promptly give the
furnisher additional relevant information provided by the consumer. If
the furnisher doesn't investigate and respond to the notification of the
dispute within the specified times, the CRA must delete the disputed
information from its files. 

FCRA 623(b)(2) and 611(a)(1) 

Hmm...What can you infer about what the law says + what you've learned
about e-OSCAR to determine how you should dispute, what you should
include in your dispute and what defenses could you used based on a
verified response...

Bruce, Factual Disputing that includes evidence of the facts is
always the best way to go! Remember, only laws get deletions. Use the
laws to your advantage.  

Furnisher Duties when Disputing with the Furnisher

When sending a "Direct Dispute" to the furnisher, the furnisher must
investigate a consumer's dispute if it relates to: 

● The consumer's liability for a credit account or other debt with
the furnisher. 
For example: disputes relating to whether there is or has been identity
theft or fraud against the consumer, whether there is individual or
joint liability on an account, or whether the consumer is an authorized
user of a credit account. Meaning, a dispute regarding who is in fact
responsible for the alleged account.

● The terms of a credit account or other debt with the furnisher. 
For example: disputes relating to the type of account, principal
balance, scheduled payment amount on an account, or the amount of the
credit limit on an open-end account; 

● The consumer's performance or other conduct concerning an account
or other relationship with the furnisher. 
For example: disputes relating to the current payment status, high
balance, date a payment was made, amount of a payment made, or date an
account was opened or closed;  

● Any other information in a consumer report about an account or
relationship with the furnisher that affects the consumer's
creditworthiness, credit standing, credit capacity, character, general
reputation, personal characteristics, or lifestyle.

Let's Review...

Whether receiving a dispute directly or indirectly, the furnisher must: 

● conduct a reasonable investigation 

● review all relevant information provided by the consumer 

● report results to the consumer, generally within 30 days 

● notify each CRA to which the furnisher has provided inaccurate
information if the investigation finds the information was inaccurate. 

When a Furnisher is NOT Required to Investigate a Dispute + Avoiding
Frivolous Dispute Responses...

Furishers are not required to investigate the dispute if it relates to: 

● the consumer's identifying information on a consumer report,
including name, date of birth, Social Security number, phone number, or
address;

● the names of previous or current employers;

● inquiries or requests for consumer reports;

● information from public records, including judgments, bankruptcies,
and liens;

● information related to fraud alerts or active duty alerts;

● information provided to a CRA by another furnisher; 

● when the furnisher has reason to believe that the dispute is
submitted by, prepared on behalf of the consumer by, or submitted on a
form supplied to the consumer by a credit repair organization.  

A furnisher is also are not required to investigate disputes that are
frivolous or irrelevant, due to any of the following reasons: 

● The consumer didn't provide enough information 

● The dispute is substantially the same as a dispute previously
submitted 

● The furnisher has already fulfilled your obligation, and there is no
new information 

If a dispute is found to be frivolous or irrelevant, the furnisher must
notify the consumer within five business days of receiving the dispute.
This notice can be a form letter. The furnisher must include the reason
for the determination and, if relevant, any information the consumer
needs to submit so the furnisher can investigate the disputed
information.

So in summary...
To avoid frivolous disputes, Always provide enough information - account
name, number, exactly what you are disputing/requesting, and the desired
action you wish to occur based on your dispute; Do not continuously
dispute the same reason; and Provide new information, which simply means
giving them another reason to investigate your dispute; 

